𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻: Your application runs in Kubernetes, but users report they cannot access it. What do you do?

Here is how you ace this question:

1. 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗹𝗼𝗴𝘀
- Run 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘨𝘦𝘵 𝘱𝘰𝘥𝘴 to verify pod status and ensure they're in Running state
- Check pod logs using 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘭𝘰𝘨𝘴 <𝘱𝘰𝘥-𝘯𝘢𝘮𝘦> to identify application errors
- Examine pod events with 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘥𝘦𝘴𝘤𝘳𝘪𝘣𝘦 𝘱𝘰𝘥 <𝘱𝘰𝘥-𝘯𝘢𝘮𝘦>

𝟮. 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲
- Check service configuration with 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘨𝘦𝘵 𝘴𝘷𝘤
- Verify service type (ClusterIP/NodePort/LoadBalancer)
- Ensure the service selector matches pod labels
- Confirm correct ports are exposed

𝟯. 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝗣𝗼𝗹𝗶𝗰𝘆
- List network policies: 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘨𝘦𝘵 𝘯𝘦𝘵𝘸𝘰𝘳𝘬𝘱𝘰𝘭𝘪𝘤𝘪𝘦𝘴
- Verify policies allow traffic to application ports
- Check if any policies are blocking incoming connections

𝟰. 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗜𝗻𝗴𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗗𝗡𝗦
- Check ingress configuration with 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘨𝘦𝘵 𝘪𝘯𝘨𝘳𝘦𝘴𝘴
- Confirm DNS records point to correct IP addresses
- Validate SSL/TLS certificates if using HTTPS

𝟱. 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗖𝗹𝘂𝘀𝘁𝗲𝗿 𝗛𝗲𝗮𝗹𝘁𝗵
- Check node status with 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘨𝘦𝘵 𝘯𝘰𝘥𝘦𝘴
- Review node resource utilization
- Examine cluster events: 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘨𝘦𝘵 𝘦𝘷𝘦𝘯𝘵𝘴

𝟲. 𝗗𝗲𝗯𝘂𝗴 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗣𝗼𝗿𝘁 𝗙𝗼𝗿𝘄𝗮𝗿𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴
- Use 𝘬𝘶𝘣𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘭 𝘱𝘰𝘳𝘵-𝘧𝘰𝘳𝘸𝘢𝘳𝘥 to test direct pod access
- This helps isolate whether the issue is with the application or Kubernetes configuration

𝟳. 𝗠𝗼𝗻𝗶𝘁𝗼𝗿 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗧𝗼𝗼𝗹𝘀
- Review metrics in Prometheus/Grafana if available
- Check the Kubernetes dashboard for cluster insights
- Look for any alerts or anomalies

𝗣𝗿𝗼 𝗧𝗶𝗽𝘀:
- Always start with the simplest checks first
- Document your troubleshooting steps
- Consider recent changes that might have impacted connectivity
